# The Code Graph

Everything you have read so far is a unit: [[score]] computes, [[enrich]] reaches
the CRM, the [[pipeline]] orchestrates, [[chart]] paints, [[forecast]] does the
heavy math, [[risk]] runs Python. Each file narrated its own job. This file does
something different — it steps back and looks at the **whole workbook as one
typed property graph**, where every unit is a node and every relationship between
units is a typed edge.

That graph is not a diagram we drew by hand. It is **extracted from the workbook
itself** and kept in sync by the weave. When you run `work check`, `work why`, or
`work near`, you are querying this graph. When a request flows through the running
app, OpenTelemetry spans ride these same nodes. The graph of the sales app *is*
the sales app, seen as structure instead of as text.

## Three extractors feed one graph

Nothing builds the graph by guessing. Three independent extractors each read a
different layer of the same source, and their outputs are merged into one store.
Disagreement between them is itself a signal — a `ref` the prose claims but the
AST cannot find is a dangling edge the gate will flag.

1. **The literate extractor** reads the prose and the markup: every `[[backlink]]`,
   every inline `:atom` and `@Type` reference, every `#tag`. When this file says
   the [[pipeline]] calls [[score]], that sentence becomes a candidate `ref` edge.
2. **The per-language AST extractor** reads the code bodies — Elixir, Rust, Zig,
   Python, Svelte — and finds the *real* call sites and dependencies. This is the
   ground truth: it sees that `Pipeline.run/1` actually invokes `Score.score/1`.
3. **The WIT extractor** reads the **generated** component contracts (the artifact
   from [[contract]] you never hand-write). Each unit's exports and imports become
   `import` edges at the wasm-component boundary — the cross-language seam.

The merge is deliberate: prose tells you *intent*, the AST tells you *fact*, and
the WIT tells you *the boundary the two will actually meet at*. A healthy node has
all three agreeing.

## The node and edge types

A node is a unit. We do not invent a new shape for it — a graph node is just a
record whose fields are inferred from their defaults, the same `defstruct` model
[[Lead]] introduced. The `kind` is one of a small atom enum, and the
`place` is the two-value placement enum the whole workbook is built on.

defmodule Node do
  defstruct id: nil,
            kind: :compute,
            place: :server,
            lang: :elixir,
            sandbox: nil,
            file: "",
            exports: [],
            grants: []
end

@kinds [:compute, :flow, :island, :data, :agent, :kit, :sandbox]
@places [:client, :server, :none]

defmodule Edge do
  defstruct from: nil, to: nil, type: :ref, via: nil
end

@edge_types [:import, :dataflow, :ref, :type, :tag]

The edge `type` is the part that makes the graph *typed* rather than a bag of
arrows. An `:import` edge is a real wasm-component dependency (extractor 3). A
`:dataflow` edge means values move — a `Lead` produced here is consumed there. A
`:ref` edge is a prose mention. A `:type` edge ties a unit to a struct or enum it
speaks (`Lead`, `@statuses`). A `:tag` edge attaches a queryable `#tag`. Same
node set, five overlaid relations.

## The graph of this workbook

Here is the graph assembled from the units you have already met. Read it as the
sales app's own wiring diagram. Note that placement and sandbox are carried on the
node, so a single query can ask structural *and* operational questions at once.

defmodule SalesGraph do
  @nodes [
    %Node{id: :lead,     kind: :data,    place: :server, lang: :elixir, file: "05-data/leads.work", exports: [:query]},
    %Node{id: :score,    kind: :compute, place: :server, lang: :elixir, file: "03-lib/score.work", exports: [:score]},
    %Node{id: :enrich,   kind: :compute, place: :server, lang: :elixir, file: "03-lib/enrich.work", exports: [:enrich], grants: [{:net, "api.crm.com"}]},
    %Node{id: :pipeline, kind: :flow,    place: :server, lang: :elixir, file: "03-lib/pipeline.work", exports: [:run]},
    %Node{id: :tax,      kind: :compute, place: :server, lang: :elixir, sandbox: :pricing, file: "04-sandboxing/pricing.work", exports: [:quote]},
    %Node{id: :fx,       kind: :compute, place: :server, lang: :elixir, sandbox: :rates, file: "04-sandboxing/pricing.work", exports: [:rate], grants: [:net]},
    %Node{id: :forecast, kind: :compute, place: :server, lang: :rust, sandbox: :forecast, file: "09-weave/forecast.work", exports: [:project]},
    %Node{id: :risk,     kind: :compute, place: :server, lang: :python, sandbox: :risk_box, file: "09-weave/risk.work", exports: [:risk_score]},
    %Node{id: :chart,    kind: :island,  place: :client, lang: :svelte, file: "09-weave/chart.work", exports: [:render]},
    %Node{id: :sparkline, kind: :island, place: :client, lang: :solid, file: "09-weave/sparkline.work", exports: [:render]},
    %Node{id: :analyst,  kind: :agent,   place: :server, lang: :elixir, file: "07-agents/analyst.work", exports: [:draft]}
  ]

  @edges [
    %Edge{from: :pipeline, to: :enrich,   type: :import},
    %Edge{from: :pipeline, to: :score,    type: :import},
    %Edge{from: :enrich,   to: :lead,     type: :dataflow, via: :Lead},
    %Edge{from: :score,    to: :lead,     type: :dataflow, via: :Lead},
    %Edge{from: :chart,    to: :lead,     type: :dataflow, via: :Lead},
    %Edge{from: :tax,      to: :fx,       type: :import, via: :host_broker},
    %Edge{from: :forecast, to: :score,    type: :dataflow},
    %Edge{from: :risk,     to: :lead,     type: :type, via: :Lead},
    %Edge{from: :analyst,  to: :score,    type: :ref},
    %Edge{from: :analyst,  to: :enrich,   type: :ref}
  ]

  def nodes, do: @nodes
  def edges, do: @edges

  def node(id), do: Enum.find(@nodes, &(&1.id == id))

  def out(id, type \\ :any) do
    @edges
    |> Enum.filter(&(&1.from == id and (type == :any or &1.type == type)))
    |> Enum.map(& &1.to)
  end

  def into(id, type \\ :any) do
    @edges
    |> Enum.filter(&(&1.to == id and (type == :any or &1.type == type)))
    |> Enum.map(& &1.from)
  end
end

## `work check` — the gate query

`work check` is the graph asking itself whether it is whole. It walks every edge
and asks the merged store to resolve both endpoints. A `:ref` edge from the prose
with no matching `:import` or `:dataflow` from the AST is a **dangling mention**;
a unit that exports a function nobody imports is a **dead node**; a cross-sandbox
edge that is not `via: :host_broker` is a **capability leak**. The weave runs this
before it bundles, which is why a red check ships no `salesapp.html`.

defmodule Check do
  alias SalesGraph, as: G

  def run do
    dangling = for e <- G.edges(), G.node(e.from) == nil or G.node(e.to) == nil, do: e

    leaks =
      for e <- G.edges(),
          e.type == :import,
          a = G.node(e.from),
          b = G.node(e.to),
          a && b && a.sandbox != b.sandbox,
          e.via != :host_broker,
          do: {e.from, e.to}

    dead =
      for n <- G.nodes(),
          n.exports != [],
          G.into(n.id) == [],
          n.kind not in [:agent, :island],
          do: n.id

    %{dangling: dangling, leaks: leaks, dead: dead}
  end
end

The cross-sandbox guard is the same rule [[tax]] and [[fx]] obey at runtime: caps
flow by **grant**, not inheritance, so any `:import` that crosses a `sandbox`
boundary must be marked host-brokered or the check refuses it. The graph encodes
the security model, not just the call structure.

## `work why` — explain a path

`work why :tax :net` answers "why does the pricing sandbox touch the network?" by
finding the path from a node to a capability. It is a typed breadth-first walk:
follow `:import` and `:dataflow` edges outward until you reach a node carrying the
grant. The answer is the chain, so the reason is legible rather than asserted.

defmodule Why do
  alias SalesGraph, as: G

  def path(from, grant) do
    walk([[from]], grant, MapSet.new([from]))
  end

  defp walk([], _grant, _seen), do: :no_path

  defp walk([[head | _] = path | rest], grant, seen) do
    node = G.node(head)

    if node && Enum.any?(node.grants, &grant_matches?(&1, grant)) do
      Enum.reverse(path)
    else
      next =
        for to <- G.out(head),
            to not in seen,
            do: [to | path]

      walk(rest ++ next, grant, MapSet.union(seen, MapSet.new(G.out(head))))
    end
  end

  defp grant_matches?(:net, :net), do: true
  defp grant_matches?({:net, _host}, :net), do: true
  defp grant_matches?(g, g), do: true
  defp grant_matches?(_, _), do: false
end

For `:tax → :net` the walk crosses the host-brokered `:import` into [[fx]], whose
node carries `grant: [:net]`, and returns `[:tax, :fx]` — the pricing sandbox
reaches the network *only* through `fx`'s rate lookup, exactly as designed. If the
path came back `:no_path`, a grant in the source is unjustified and should be
removed; the graph turns least-privilege into a checkable property.

## `work near` — the neighbourhood

`work near :lead` returns the units that touch the `Lead` struct — the blast
radius of a type change. Because edges are typed we can scope the question:
`:dataflow` neighbours move `Lead` values; `:type` neighbours merely speak the
shape. Both matter when you add a field to [[Lead]].

defmodule Near do
  alias SalesGraph, as: G

  def of(id, hops \\ 1) do
    expand(MapSet.new([id]), hops)
    |> MapSet.delete(id)
    |> Enum.sort()
  end

  defp expand(frontier, 0), do: frontier

  defp expand(frontier, hops) do
    grown =
      Enum.reduce(frontier, frontier, fn id, acc ->
        acc
        |> MapSet.union(MapSet.new(G.out(id)))
        |> MapSet.union(MapSet.new(G.into(id)))
      end)

    if MapSet.equal?(grown, frontier), do: grown, else: expand(grown, hops - 1)
  end
end

`Near.of(:lead)` yields `[:chart, :enrich, :risk, :score]` — every unit that would
need a glance if `Lead` grew a field. That is the practical payoff of the graph: a
change's reach is a query, not a memory of who-touches-what.

## OpenTelemetry rides the nodes

The graph is not only a build-time artifact. The same node ids are the **span
names** the runtime emits. When the [[pipeline]] runs on the nexus, each unit it
calls opens a span keyed by its node id, so a trace in production is a *walk of the
build-time graph* with live latencies hung on it. The structural edge
`pipeline → enrich` and the runtime span `pipeline → enrich (412ms)` are the same
edge, observed two ways.

defmodule Trace do
  alias SalesGraph, as: G

  def span(node_id, fun) do
    n = G.node(node_id)

    attrs = %{
      "wb.kind" => n.kind,
      "wb.place" => n.place,
      "wb.lang" => n.lang,
      "wb.sandbox" => n.sandbox
    }

    :otel_tracer.with_span(node_id, %{attributes: attrs}, fn _ctx ->
      fun.()
    end)
  end
end

Because `place` and `sandbox` ride on every span, a latency outlier is immediately
attributable to a tier: a slow `:client` island is a hydration problem, a slow
`sandbox: :rates` span is the `fx` network grant, a slow native-server span is the
BEAM. The code graph is the join key between *how the workbook is built* and *how
it behaves* — one typed graph, queried at the gate by `work check`, explained by
`work why`, scoped by `work near`, and observed in flight by every span.
